rt button has  been cautiously pushed to get the oil  flowing this will not be an immediate  fix and it will take some time to resume  normal operations the pipeline normally  ships 300 000 barrels of oil per day  from alberta to british columbia for use  here and in washington state and to  export overseas but the floods three  weeks ago forced tmx to shut it down to  check for any leaks or breaks  that had a domino effect on the  refineries with no oil coming in no gas  went out i would be surprised if they  hadn't drawn down their reserves to too  close to nothing especially as they had  to shut down the refinery  that has trickled down to drivers the  province limited each fill up to 30  liters at first it sparked panic at the  pumps then something interesting  happened at gas stations when the  rationing first started the average  price of regular fuel was a dollar  sixty-two per liter but it started  dropping and at one point in december it  was a dollar fifty-three  gas price watchers attributed to british  columbians quickly appreciating the  reality of the crisis there was no  gouging  there was no profiteering there was  instead a concerted effort to  everyone looking after everyone's  interests the situation may serve as a  lesson for the province to start storing  greater reserves in case of future  emergencies like earthquakes it's  significant because when we are looking  at the supplies we only have that much  product stored and eventually will run  out of it and  luckily we have avoided disaster the  pipeline will take at least a week  before it's back operating at a hundred  percent then it'll take more time for  the refinery to ramp back up so that  still leaves limits on how much gas  drivers can get at least until december  14th robin gill global news vancouver  ahead indonesians search for the dead  after a devastating volcanic eruption  severe weather is walloping the hawaiian  islands forecasters say the storm system  could drop over 60 centimeters of rain  in some areas there are flash flood  warnings in effect for several islands  including oahu maui and the big island  and there is a blizzard warning for  hawaii's highest summits the storm is  expected to last until tomorrow the u.s  national weather service warns the storm  could produce catastrophic flooding and  strong winds  in indonesia the death toll is rising  after a devastating volcanic eruption on  java island at least 22 people are  confirmed to have died dozens of others  are still missing the volcano erupted  saturday burying towns and villages  crews have been digging through the mud  searching for victims people in the area  have been told to stay away and the  situation remains highly volatile  because the volcano is still erupting  a mountain climber has been given the  jewels he found on a french glacier more  than 50 years after they were lost in a  plane crash the treasure trove of  emeralds rubies and sapphires was buried  for decades near mont blanc in france  they're believed to have belonged to a  passenger killed in one of two air india  flights that crashed in 1950 and 1966  a mountaineer stumbled across the gems  in 2013 he turned them in authorities  say efforts to track down the owner's  family failed the precious stones worth  nearly half a million dollars will now  be shared between the climber and local  authorities  returning to merit bc next the  frustrations after a flooding  catastrophe  in the coming weeks many of us will be  home for the holidays in parts of  british columbia that's going to be  difficult if not impossible after being  forced out by flooding and weeks of  extreme weather people in merit are  returning to a community in need of a  multitude of repairs mike drale reports  they may have hoped for the best when  they were forced to leave everything  behind three weeks ago but the residents  of merit bc knew deep down they'd be  returning to a changed city and when  they entered their homes they barely  recognized them for what they were i  just got back this last this weekend and  everything was frozen there's mud at the  bottom of the stairs and i couldn't even  shovel out the damage to this south  central city was extensive roads and  bridges were wiped out the water and  sewage stopped working and the hospital  was shut down  the city as officials so bluntly put it  wasn't safe  yet residents were eager to return  because water damage compounds over time  you want to be in here right away just  to get all the water and moisture out so  you don't get molds in behind the walls  it's fair to say residents from the  fraser valley up into the interior could  use a breaker too but winter isn't  really helping the snow may look nice  but it's a superficial mass to problems  that won't go away the one intractable  fact is the scope of the disaster  there's only so many electricians or  plumbers or construction workers  especially for more remote communities  and there's certainly not enough to help  everyone in a hurry had no information i  don't even know if my water's on or off  i don't know how to get a hold of  anybody to get the water off if it is on  you know i mean if if the pipes burst  and then it thaws again  then i'll have more water in my basement  and how can they not be frustrated the  city has deemed  366 homes unlivable a number that is  almost certain to rise  the flood of 2021 may be seeing its  waters drain out of merit but the good  people who live there still feel like  they're underwater mike dralet global  news  and that is global national for this  monday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is park and tilford gardens in  north vancouver bc we'd love to see your  canada especially festive and joyful  ones this time of year please email your  photo to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]  you
 

